package com.poshith.intent.core

/*
 * The Mind library: short lessons, each tied to a real source, with an honest label for how strong
 * the evidence is. It's finite on purpose. You can finish it; it's not another feed.
 */

enum class Evidence(val label: String) {
    STRONG("Strong evidence"),
    GOOD("Good evidence"),
    EARLY("Early evidence"),
    MIXED("Mixed evidence"),
    STORY("Real-world example"),
}

enum class Topic(val title: String, val blurb: String) {
    HOOK("How phones hook us", "The mechanics behind the pull, minus the scare stories."),
    EVIDENCE("What the research says", "What actually helped people in experiments."),
    MYTHS("Myths, checked", "Popular ideas about the mind and success, against what the research found."),
    MINDS("How sharp minds use phones", "What well-known builders, investors and scientists do."),
    BRAIN("A better brain", "Attention, boredom, sleep, movement."),
    PRACTICE("Small practices", "Things to try today. One at a time."),
}

class Lesson(
    val id: String,
    val topic: Topic,
    val title: String,
    val body: String,
    val tryThis: String,
    val source: String,
    val evidence: Evidence,
)

object Lessons {

    val all: List<Lesson> = listOf(
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------ how phones hook us
        Lesson("h-self", Topic.HOOK, "Most pickups are your idea, not your phone's",
            "Researchers at LSE filmed 37 people's days with small wearable cameras. Of 1,130 phone interactions, 89% were started by the person, not by a notification, and they came about every five minutes in every kind of situation. Many were so automatic that people didn't remember picking the phone up.",
            "Next time your hand reaches for the phone, notice what you felt a second before.",
            "Heitmayer & Lahlou (2021), Computers in Human Behavior", Evidence.GOOD),
        Lesson("h-count", Topic.HOOK, "You check about twice as often as you think",
            "When researchers logged real phone use for two weeks, young adults used their phones about 85 times a day, around five hours in total, and more than half of those uses lasted under 30 seconds. People's guesses about how often they checked didn't match what they actually did.",
            "Look at your unlock count on the home screen once today. Only look; no judging.",
            "Andrews, Ellis, Shaw & Piwek (2015), PLOS ONE", Evidence.GOOD),
        Lesson("h-slot", Topic.HOOK, "The slot machine in your pocket",
            "Rewards that arrive unpredictably (a like, a reply, a funny video) are the hardest ones to stop chasing. That is how slot machines work. A study of more than a million social-media posts found people's posting followed the same reward-learning pattern. Pull-to-refresh is the lever.",
            "Notice the little lift of 'maybe something new'. That feeling is the hook, not the content.",
            "Lindström et al. (2021), Nature Communications", Evidence.GOOD),
        Lesson("h-wanting", Topic.HOOK, "Wanting is not liking",
            "Brain research separates 'wanting' (the pull, driven largely by dopamine) from 'liking' (the pleasure you actually feel). The two can come apart: a strong urge to check, and little joy once you do. That gap is why scrolling can feel compulsive and empty at the same time.",
            "After your next scroll, ask: did I enjoy that, or did I only want it?",
            "Berridge & Robinson (2016), American Psychologist. Applying it to phones is an interpretation.", Evidence.GOOD),
        Lesson("h-scroll", Topic.HOOK, "Feeds have no bottom on purpose",
            "Aza Raskin designed infinite scroll in 2006 to make browsing easier. He has since said he regrets it, estimating that it wastes the equivalent of hundreds of thousands of human lifetimes every day. With no natural stopping point, the decision to stop is left to you, usually when you're most tired.",
            "Decide where you'll stop before you start. Intent's session length does exactly this.",
            "Aza Raskin, interviews (BBC 2018; ReThinking with Adam Grant, 2024)", Evidence.STORY),
        Lesson("h-cues", Topic.HOOK, "Cues move your hand before you decide",
            "A colourful icon, a red badge, a dull moment in a queue: repeated often enough, each becomes a trigger. Removing the cue is easier than resisting it every time. In a randomized trial, a bundle of simple nudges like these reduced problematic phone use and screen time.",
            "Keep only tools on the home screen. Reach everything else by typing.",
            "Olson et al. (2022), randomized trial; Center for Humane Technology, Take Control", Evidence.GOOD),
        Lesson("h-loop", Topic.HOOK, "Name the feeling before the reach",
            "Habits run on a loop: a feeling (bored, stressed, lonely), then a craving, then your hand on the phone. Cognitive neuroscientist Sahar Yousef recommends stepping in at the feeling, by asking yourself what you feel. Lab studies back the idea: putting a feeling into words calmed the amygdala, the brain's alarm centre.",
            "When the pull comes, name it in one word. Intent's pause asks you exactly this.",
            "Dr. Sahar Yousef (UC Berkeley), Figuring Out podcast; Lieberman et al. (2007), Psychological Science", Evidence.GOOD),
        Lesson("h-candy", Topic.HOOK, "Scrolling when lonely is candy when hungry",
            "Dr. Yousef's analogy: scrolling when you feel lonely gives a quick hit but not the nourishment of real contact, so you can end up feeling emptier. The analogy is hers; the evidence that fits it is a trial in which students who cut social media to about 30 minutes a day became less lonely.",
            "Feeling lonely? Call or text one real person before opening a feed.",
            "Dr. Sahar Yousef; Hunt et al. (2018), J. of Social and Clinical Psychology", Evidence.GOOD),
        Lesson("h-gap", Topic.HOOK, "Calm you underestimates craving you",
            "When we're calm, we underestimate how strong a future urge will feel: the 'hot-cold empathy gap'. People most sure of their self-control also put themselves in the way of temptation more, and gave in more: in one study, ex-smokers who felt surest of themselves were more likely to have started again months later. That's why limits work best when you set them while calm, and can't undo them in the moment.",
            "Set tomorrow's limits tonight, while you're calm.",
            "Loewenstein (2005), Health Psychology; Nordgren, van Harreveld & van der Pligt (2009), Psychological Science", Evidence.GOOD),
